      <header>
        <h1>Tolerance by the Numbers</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">
          What the data says about the paradox of tolerance, the scale of intolerance, and what justice actually looks like when societies come out the other side.
        </p>
        <p class="meta">60 min read</p>
      </header>

      <article>

        <p>
          I am tolerant. I want to increase global tolerance. I want justice. And I am angry that I am not getting my way.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is not a comfortable starting point for an essay. It lacks the detachment that data analysis is supposed to provide. But I have come to believe that detachment is part of the problem. The YouTubers and Substackers I follow are informed and eloquent. They speak frequently and at length. They are an invaluable resource. But mainly I walk away from them with anger. A more informed anger, yes. An anger that might be channeled into action. But it remains vague. If I think critically about it, there are missing parts to the message. The part where anger becomes strategy. The part where strategy becomes pragmatic action grounded in data.
        </p>

        <p>
          There is love and poetry and song and the refusal to worship the dogma of human rationality. Those things matter enormously. I have written about them and spoken about them. But that is not what this essay is. This essay is about numbers. Cold, unforgiving, sometimes horrifying numbers. I want the numbers to help me think more correctly, and at the very least, to start asking myself more pragmatic questions about what tolerance, intolerance, and justice actually look like at scale.
        </p>

        <p>
          Here is what I found. The number of people who support intolerant movements is much larger than most tolerant people want to believe. The number of perpetrators who face consequences after intolerant regimes fall is much smaller than anyone imagines. The time it takes for societies to recover from periods of intolerance is measured in generations, not elections. And the mechanisms that actually work to build and restore tolerance are unglamorous, local, institutional, and incremental. None of these findings are what I wanted to find. All of them are what the data shows. And if we are serious about increasing tolerance, we need to start from where the data says we are, not where we wish we were.
        </p>

        <p>
          A note on method. Every number in this essay is sourced from publicly available data. Voter tallies come from the Associated Press and German federal election archives. Genocide death tolls come from the United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, R.J. Rummel's democide research at the University of Hawaii, and the work of historians like Frank Dikotter and Mary Fulbrook. Democracy scores come from Freedom House, the V-Dem Institute, and the Economist Intelligence Unit. Immigration enforcement data comes from TRAC at Syracuse University and CBS News. I have not synthesized, estimated, or extrapolated any figures. Where scholars disagree on numbers, I use midpoint estimates and note the range. The sources are cited beneath each chart and compiled in full at the end of the essay. If a number in this essay is wrong, it is because the source was wrong, and I welcome corrections.
        </p>

        <h2>The First Fallacy: Underestimating the Intolerant</h2>

        <p>
          The philosopher Karl Popper articulated the paradox of tolerance in 1945: unlimited tolerance must lead to the disappearance of tolerance. If we extend unlimited tolerance even to those who are intolerant, if we are not prepared to defend a tolerant society against the onslaught of the intolerant, then the tolerant will be destroyed and tolerance with them. Popper was writing in the shadow of the very movement that proved his point. The Nazis did not seize power against the will of the German people. They were voted in. Not by a majority, but by enough.
        </p>

        <p>
          The first fallacy tolerant people commit is underplaying the absolute number of intolerant people. It is psychologically easier to imagine that intolerance is a fringe phenomenon, that the people who support authoritarian movements are a small, loud minority who will naturally be overcome by the decent majority. The numbers say otherwise.
        </p>

        <p>
          In the 2024 United States presidential election, Donald Trump received 77,303,573 votes. This was the second-highest vote total in American history, trailing only Joe Biden's 81.3 million in 2020. These 77 million people voted for a candidate who had been convicted of 34 felonies, who had attempted to overturn the results of the previous election, who had promised mass deportations, and who had been endorsed by white nationalist organizations. Not every one of those 77 million voters is a white nationalist. But every one of them decided that white nationalism was not a dealbreaker.
        </p>

        <p>
          Now consider the historical comparison that haunts this entire discussion. In March 1933, the Nationalsozialistische Deutsche Arbeiterpartei, the National Socialist German Workers' Party, received 17,277,180 votes in the last semi-competitive German federal election. That was 43.9 percent of the vote, enough to give Adolf Hitler the parliamentary leverage he needed to pass the Enabling Act and dismantle the Weimar Republic. Those 17 million voters are the people we now look back on and ask: how could they?
        </p>

        <Chart chartId="tolerance-maga-vs-nazi" title="Peak Voter Support: MAGA vs. Nazi Party" caption="Trump 2024 vote total from AP/Cook Political Report certified count (Dec 2024). NSDAP vote total from March 1933 German federal election records. The March 1933 election occurred after Hitler was already Chancellor and amid SA violence and press suppression." />

        <p class="source-note">Sources: Associated Press certified results (Dec 30, 2024); German federal election records via Bundesarchiv; Cook Political Report 2024 National Popular Vote Tracker.</p>

        <p>
          The chart demands a moment of silence. The MAGA movement's voter base is four and a half times larger than the Nazi Party's peak electoral support. Four and a half times. The raw numbers matter because they determine what is possible. Seventeen million people enabled the Holocaust. They enabled the murder of six million Jews, three million Soviet prisoners of war, hundreds of thousands of Roma, disabled people, political prisoners, and others. Seventeen million votes was the mandate for the industrialization of death.
        </p>

        <p>
          I am not arguing that MAGA and the Nazi Party are morally equivalent in their actions. The Trump administration has not built extermination camps. But I am arguing that the scale comparison matters for a different reason: it tells us something about the scope of the problem anyone working toward tolerance must contend with. If 17 million intolerant voters could enable the worst atrocity in modern European history, what are 77 million intolerant voters capable of enabling? We do not know the answer yet. We are living inside the experiment.
        </p>

        <p>
          The comparison becomes even more stark when you move beyond voters to supporters. Not everyone who backs an authoritarian movement votes. Pew Research Center found in January 2026 that 27 percent of all American adults, roughly 64 million people, say they support all or most of Trump's policies. Broader approval polls put the number who view Trump favorably at somewhere between 88 and 100 million adults, depending on the pollster and the phrasing of the question. Germany's registered electorate in 1933 was 44.7 million. The total population was 67 million. The MAGA-sympathetic population in the United States today exceeds the entire population of 1933 Germany.
        </p>

        <p>
          There is a tempting objection here, and I want to address it head-on. Someone reading this will say: "You cannot compare MAGA voters to Nazi voters. The situations are completely different. America in 2024 is not Weimar Germany. Trump is not Hitler." All of those statements are true in important ways. America in 2024 has vastly stronger democratic institutions than Weimar Germany. Trump does not have a paramilitary force equivalent to the Sturmabteilung (SA). The historical, cultural, and institutional contexts are profoundly different.
        </p>

        <p>
          But the comparison is not about equivalence of outcomes. It is about scale of participation. The point is not that 77 million Americans will enable a Holocaust. The point is that 77 million is a staggeringly large number of people who have demonstrated a willingness to support leaders who undermine democratic norms, target vulnerable minorities, and concentrate executive power. When we think about what it takes to resist intolerance, we need to grapple with the fact that we are not dealing with a fringe. We are dealing with roughly a third of the adult population of the most powerful country on earth. The strategies appropriate for countering a fringe, things like social pressure, public shaming, or marginalization, do not work when a third of the country is on the other side. You cannot shame 77 million people into changing their minds. You cannot marginalize a third of the electorate. You need a different playbook, and finding that playbook requires first accepting the scale of the problem.
        </p>

        <p>
          Consider, too, that the March 1933 election was not a free and fair contest. Hitler was already Chancellor. The Reichstag had burned six days earlier. The SA was terrorizing opposition politicians and voters. Thousands of Communists and Social Democrats had been arrested. The press was being censored. Under those conditions, the Nazi Party managed only 43.9 percent. In a free election in July 1932, they had peaked at 37.3 percent with 13.7 million votes. By November 1932, support had already declined to 33.1 percent. Many observers at the time believed the Nazi movement had peaked and was in decline. They were wrong, but the point is that even under the best conditions for mobilizing intolerant voters, the Nazis never achieved majority support. They came to power through the institutional failures of Weimar, through the Enabling Act, through elite complicity, through the inadequacy of democratic guardrails. The lesson is not that intolerant majorities seize power. The lesson is that intolerant minorities seize power when institutions fail to stop them. And 77 million, while not a majority, is a far larger minority than the Nazis ever had.
        </p>

        <h2>The Impunity Ratio</h2>

        <p>
          Now let us talk about what happened after the Nazis lost power. This is the part of the story that tolerant people like to tell ourselves: the Allies won, the Nuremberg Trials held the perpetrators to account, and justice was served. It makes for an inspiring narrative. It is also almost entirely false.
        </p>

        <p>
          At the International Military Tribunal in Nuremberg, 21 senior Nazi leaders were tried. Nineteen were convicted. Twelve were sentenced to death. In the twelve subsequent Nuremberg trials, 177 more defendants faced justice. One hundred forty-two were convicted. Combined, the entire Nuremberg process convicted 161 people.
        </p>

        <p>
          One hundred sixty-one people. Out of 17 million voters. Out of 8.5 million Nazi Party members. Out of the hundreds of thousands who actively participated in the machinery of the Holocaust. Let those numbers sit together for a moment.
        </p>

        <Chart chartId="tolerance-nazi-voters-vs-convicted" title="Nazi Voters vs. Post-War Convictions" caption="NSDAP voters from March 1933 German federal election. Conviction total includes all post-WWII courts worldwide: Nuremberg (161), West German courts (6,656), East German courts (12,890), other European and Soviet courts (est. 100,000 total)." />

        <p class="source-note">Sources: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum; The Holocaust Explained (Wiener Holocaust Library); Mary Fulbrook, Reckonings: Legacies of Nazi Persecution and the Quest for Justice (Oxford, 2018).</p>

        <p>
          The chart shows two bars that should not be on the same graph. They are separated by a factor of 173. For every Nazi voter, 0.006 people were convicted of anything. The broader conviction count across all courts worldwide, approximately 100,000 people over several decades, sounds large in isolation. It is less than 1.2 percent of the Nazi Party's membership. Historian Mary Fulbrook has documented that West German courts, which operated for decades after the war, convicted only 6,656 people out of 140,000 who had cases brought against them. Only 164 of those convictions were for murder. In East Germany, 12,890 were found guilty, but the majority received prison terms of less than three years.
        </p>

        <p>
          Let me put this differently. If you were an active participant in the Nazi regime, a card-carrying party member who looked the other way while your Jewish neighbors were deported, who staffed the bureaucracy that processed the trains, who managed the factories that used slave labor, your odds of facing any legal consequence after the war were roughly one in a hundred. Your odds of facing a serious consequence, meaning real prison time, were roughly one in a thousand. Your odds of being executed were roughly one in fifty thousand. Those are not the odds of justice. Those are the odds of being struck by lightning.
        </p>

        <Chart chartId="tolerance-perpetrator-gap" title="The Impunity Funnel: Nazi Germany's Perpetrators vs. Justice" caption="Estimated 200,000-1,000,000 people actively participated in Holocaust killings (Fulbrook). Only a fraction faced investigation, and fewer still faced serious prison sentences." />

        <p class="source-note">Sources: Mary Fulbrook, Reckonings (2018); USHMM Holocaust Encyclopedia; The Holocaust Explained (Wiener Holocaust Library); National WWII Museum Nuremberg Trials documentation.</p>

        <p>
          The impunity funnel narrows with terrifying speed. Half a million people are estimated to have actively participated in killing during the Holocaust. That is a conservative figure from Fulbrook's research, counting only those directly involved in shooting, gassing, or otherwise causing death. One hundred forty thousand were investigated. One hundred thousand were convicted, across every court in every country over a span of decades. Six thousand six hundred fifty-six received meaningful sentences in West Germany. Two hundred were executed. At each stage of the funnel, justice evaporates. The vast majority of perpetrators lived out their lives in peace. Many prospered. Some held government office in the postwar Federal Republic of Germany. The 1951 Article 131 law actively reinstated former Nazi civil servants into government positions.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is not ancient history. This is the best-case scenario. The Holocaust is the most documented atrocity in human history. It occurred in a country that was occupied by the victorious powers, who had every incentive and ability to pursue accountability. It was followed by decades of prosecutions. And still, fewer than one percent of perpetrators faced consequences. What does this tell us about the prospects for accountability in situations where the documentation is less thorough, the international pressure weaker, and the political will more fragile?
        </p>

        <h2>The Unthinkable Thought Experiment</h2>

        <p>
          Now I want to engage in a thought experiment that is deliberately disturbing. If 77 million Americans voted for an intolerant movement, and if that movement eventually loses power, what does justice look like? Let us explore the most extreme possibilities first so that we can appreciate why the pragmatic answers are so much less satisfying than we want them to be.
        </p>

        <p>
          We certainly do not want to murder 88 million United States citizens. I use 88 million rather than 77 million because it approximates the broader base of people who approve of Trump's agenda, including non-voters. We are imagining this not because it is desirable but because placing it alongside history's worst atrocities reveals the absurdity of retributive fantasies.
        </p>

        <Chart chartId="tolerance-genocide-comparison" title="Deadliest Genocides and Democides in History" caption="Death tolls use midpoint of scholarly estimates. The imagined 88M figure is included to illustrate scale only. China under Mao includes Great Leap Forward famine (est. 30-55M), Cultural Revolution, and other campaigns. Nazi Germany includes Holocaust plus all other targeted killings." />

        <p class="source-note">Sources: R.J. Rummel, 20th Century Democide; USHMM; Frank Dikotter, Mao's Great Famine (2010); UCLA Cambodian Genocide Program; UN Outreach Programme on the 1994 Genocide against the Tutsi in Rwanda.</p>

        <p>
          The red bar on the chart is the imagined genocide. It would be the largest mass killing in recorded history, exceeding even the highest estimates for Mao Zedong's democide in China, which killed an estimated 40 to 80 million people between 1949 and 1976 through the Great Leap Forward famine, the Cultural Revolution, and other campaigns. It would dwarf the Holocaust by a factor of five. It would exceed the estimated death toll of Stalin's Soviet Union by a factor of seven. It would be roughly 110 times the Rwandan genocide, in which 800,000 Tutsi were killed in a hundred days.
        </p>

        <p>
          The thought experiment is repulsive, and it should be. That is exactly the point. When tolerant people fantasize about punishing the intolerant, we are imagining something that, at the scale required, would itself be the greatest atrocity in human history. The moral logic collapses. You cannot murder your way to tolerance. The numbers make this obvious even when the emotions do not.
        </p>

        <p>
          We also certainly do not want to detain 88 million people. Consider the logistical and moral dimensions of detention at scale.
        </p>

        <Chart chartId="tolerance-detention" title="Scale of MAGA Targeting vs. Actual Enforcement" caption="Compares the populations MAGA rhetoric targets, the actual Trump voter base, the estimated undocumented population, and the current scale of immigration enforcement. ICE detention figure from Jan 2026 (CBS News). Deportation figure is TRAC independent estimate for calendar year 2025." />

        <p class="source-note">Sources: U.S. Census Bureau (2024 ACS); AP certified 2024 election results; Pew Research Center unauthorized immigrant estimates (Aug 2025); TRAC at Syracuse University immigration reports; CBS News (Jan 2026).</p>

        <p>
          The chart reveals an irony that is almost mathematical. The Trump administration's immigration apparatus, the most aggressive enforcement machinery deployed in modern American history, has managed to detain approximately 73,000 people at any one time and deport roughly 335,000 in its first year. That is against an estimated undocumented population of 14 million, itself a subset of the 112 million Black and Hispanic Americans whose communities bear the weight of MAGA rhetoric. The state's actual capacity for systematic cruelty, while devastating to the individuals caught in it, is orders of magnitude smaller than either its ambitions or its targets.
        </p>

        <p>
          The numbers inside the immigration enforcement system deserve closer scrutiny because they reveal the gap between authoritarian rhetoric and authoritarian capability. The administration set a target of 1 million deportations per year. Border czar Tom Homan later revised this to 600,000 for 2025. The actual number, according to TRAC at Syracuse University, which independently tracks Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) data, was approximately 335,000. The Department of Homeland Security (DHS) claims a much higher figure, roughly 675,000 deportations plus 2.2 million "self-deportations," a category that cannot be independently verified and that NPR's investigation described as "not accurate." The gap between the administration's claims and the independently verified numbers is itself a data point worth noting. Authoritarian movements consistently exaggerate the scale of their enforcement because the appearance of omnipotent state power is itself a tool of control.
        </p>

        <p>
          Within the detention system, the human cost is concentrated on the people least able to bear it. Of the 65,135 people in ICE detention as of November 2025, 47,964 of them, 74 percent, had never been convicted of any criminal offense. They were detained for the administrative violation of being in the country without authorization, a civil matter, not a criminal one. ICE expanded from roughly 120 facilities to 225 facilities in 2025, a 91 percent increase that included reopened prisons and tent facilities on military bases. And 2025 was the deadliest year for ICE detention on record. These are the consequences of intolerance in action. They are devastating. They are also, by the standards of historical authoritarian projects, remarkably limited in scope. Which brings us back to the original question.
        </p>

        <p>
          And here is the deeper irony. If we imagine the reverse scenario, a tolerant majority attempting to systematically detain 88 million MAGA supporters, we are imagining an operation that would make the current immigration enforcement look like a parking violation. The United States prison system, the largest in the world, holds 1.9 million people. Detaining 88 million would require building 46 times the current prison capacity. You would need to construct the equivalent of the entire American prison-industrial complex, a system that took decades and hundreds of billions of dollars to build, 46 times over. You would need to feed, house, and guard a population larger than Germany or Turkey. You would need a guard-to-prisoner ratio that would consume the entire American labor force. The logistics alone are a reductio ad absurdum. Mass incarceration as a tool for enforcing tolerance is not just morally bankrupt but practically impossible.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is not an academic point. It matters because the fantasy of retribution, the belief that after the intolerant lose power we can simply round them up and punish them, is deeply embedded in how tolerant people think about justice. It is the mirror image of the authoritarian's fantasy about rounding up immigrants. Both fantasies share the same flaw: they imagine that state violence at scale is a precision instrument rather than what it actually is, which is a blunt and indiscriminate machine that destroys everything in its path, including the values of the people who wield it.
        </p>

        <p>
          So if we cannot kill them, and we cannot imprison them, what do we do with 88 million people who have chosen intolerance? This question is not new. It is the question every society that has emerged from authoritarianism has had to answer. And the historical answers are deeply unsatisfying.
        </p>

        <h2>Measuring Intolerance: Where America Stands</h2>

        <p>
          Before examining how other societies have exited periods of intolerance, we need to establish where the United States currently stands. Fortunately, political scientists have been building measurement tools for decades. Two of the most respected are Freedom House's Freedom in the World index and the Varieties of Democracy (V-Dem) project based at the University of Gothenburg in Sweden.
        </p>

        <p>
          Freedom House rates every country on a scale from 0 to 100, where 100 represents the most free. The index measures political rights and civil liberties across 25 indicators, including electoral process, political pluralism, government function, freedom of expression, associational and organizational rights, rule of law, and personal autonomy. It has been published annually since 1973 and is considered one of the gold standards in comparative democracy measurement.
        </p>

        <p>
          The V-Dem project takes a different approach. It uses expert surveys from over 3,700 country specialists to construct multiple indices of democracy, including electoral democracy, liberal democracy, participatory democracy, deliberative democracy, and egalitarian democracy. Its Liberal Democracy Index, scored from 0 to 1, is particularly useful because it measures not just whether elections happen but whether the institutional infrastructure that makes elections meaningful, things like independent courts, press freedom, and minority protections, actually functions.
        </p>

        <Chart chartId="tolerance-freedom-house" title="Freedom House Scores: Where the US Stands (2025)" caption="Freedom in the World 2025 scores (0-100). The United States scores 84, placing it below most Western democracies and alongside Romania and Croatia. Countries below 35 are classified as 'Not Free.' Blue = current US, yellow = democratic backsliding, red = authoritarian." />

        <p class="source-note">Sources: Freedom House, Freedom in the World 2025 report (February 2025).</p>

        <p>
          The chart tells a story about company. The United States, with a score of 84, is no longer in the neighborhood of its traditional peers. Finland scores 100. Sweden scores 99. Norway, New Zealand, Canada, Denmark, and the Netherlands all score 97 or above. The US at 84 sits closer to Hungary at 66 than to any of those countries. Hungary under Viktor Orban is the poster child for democratic backsliding in Europe, a country that Freedom House downgraded from "Free" to "Partly Free" in 2019. The distance between the US and Hungary is 18 points. The distance between the US and Canada is 13 points. America is drifting away from the democratic world it helped build.
        </p>

        <Chart chartId="tolerance-us-decline" title="US Freedom House Score (2006-2025)" caption="The US peaked at 94 in 2010 and has declined 10 points since, with the steepest drops during Trump's first term (2017-2020). The decline from 94 to 83 represents a loss of 11 points over a decade, unprecedented among established democracies." />

        <p class="source-note">Sources: Freedom House, Freedom in the World annual reports (2006-2025).</p>

        <p>
          The US Freedom House trajectory shows a decline that began before Trump and accelerated during his first term. The country scored 94 in 2010 and dropped to 83 by 2020, a loss of 11 points in a single decade. That might not sound dramatic until you realize that an 11-point decline is roughly the difference between Denmark and Greece, or between France and Senegal. Established democracies are not supposed to move this far this fast. The score stabilized at 83-84 during the Biden years but did not recover. And in September 2025, the V-Dem project went further than Freedom House. Its director, Staffan Lindberg, declared that the United States "no longer qualifies as a democracy," reclassifying it as an electoral autocracy, the same category as Hungary, Turkey, and Serbia.
        </p>

        <p>
          That reclassification is contested. Reasonable people can argue about where the threshold between "flawed democracy" and "electoral autocracy" lies. But the direction is not contested. Every major democracy index shows the United States moving in the same direction: away from its democratic peers and toward the authoritarian world. Freedom House, V-Dem, the Economist Intelligence Unit, which downgraded the US from "full democracy" to "flawed democracy" in 2016, all tell the same story. The 2025 Century Foundation Democracy Meter rated the US at 57 out of 100, a 28 percent drop from 79 just one year earlier. More than half the decline was in state institutions, which fell from 22 out of 30 to 10 out of 30.
        </p>

        <p>
          Now look at the global picture. The World Values Survey, conducted in waves since 1981, asks people in dozens of countries whether they would want a neighbor of a different race. The answers provide a raw, unfiltered measure of racial intolerance.
        </p>

        <Chart chartId="tolerance-world-values" title="Racial Intolerance: Would Not Want a Neighbor of a Different Race" caption="World Values Survey Wave 7 (2017-2022). The question measures revealed preference for racial segregation. The United States scores relatively well on this measure at 5%, though this figure represents approximately 12 million adults." />

        <p class="source-note">Sources: World Values Survey Wave 7 (2017-2022); King's College London, Love Thy Neighbour report; Washington Post analysis (2013, updated with Wave 7 data).</p>

        <p>
          The United States actually scores relatively well on this particular measure, with only 5 percent saying they would not want a neighbor of a different race. That puts it behind Sweden (1 percent) and the United Kingdom (2 percent) but well ahead of India (43.5 percent), Jordan (51.4 percent), and Bangladesh (71.7 percent). But "relatively well" and "well" are different things. Five percent of American adults is approximately 12 million people who will openly tell a pollster they do not want to live near someone of a different race. The actual number who hold such views but know not to say so is certainly higher. And racial intolerance is only one dimension of the problem. Intolerance of immigrants, of LGBTQ people, of religious minorities, of political opponents, each adds its own layer.
        </p>

        <p>
          The global context is even more sobering. Freedom House's 2025 report found that 2024 marked the nineteenth consecutive year of decline in global freedom. The world has been getting less free every year since 2006. Autocracies now outnumber democracies for the first time in two decades. According to V-Dem, only 12 percent of the world's population, roughly 960 million people, lives in a liberal democracy. Seventy-two percent, 5.8 billion people, live under some form of autocratic rule. The tolerant are not the silent majority. Globally, the tolerant are the minority.
        </p>

        <p>
          This deserves emphasis because it cuts against one of the most comforting narratives in liberal thought: the idea that history bends toward justice, that the arc of progress is long but fundamentally upward. The data says the opposite. For nearly two decades, the global trend has been toward less freedom, less tolerance, less democracy. The brief period of democratic expansion that followed the fall of the Soviet Union, what political scientist Samuel Huntington called the "third wave" of democratization, peaked in the early 2000s and has been receding ever since. What we are experiencing is not an aberration. It is a trend. And trends, unlike aberrations, require sustained effort to reverse.
        </p>

        <p>
          The Economist Intelligence Unit's Democracy Index, which has been tracking the health of democracies since 2006, downgraded the United States from "full democracy" to "flawed democracy" in its 2016 report. That classification has not changed. The US score in 2024 was 7.85 out of 10, well below the 8.01 threshold for "full democracy." Americans tend to think of their country as the world's leading democracy. By every major international measure, it has not been one for nearly a decade.
        </p>

        <h2>How Germany Exited</h2>

        <p>
          Germany's exit from Nazism is the case study everyone reaches for, and it is instructive, but not for the reasons people think. The popular narrative goes like this: the Allies won the war, held the Nuremberg Trials, conducted denazification, and Germany became a model democracy. Each of those steps happened. None of them worked the way we imagine.
        </p>

        <p>
          Denazification was the Allied program to purge Nazi ideology and personnel from German public life. In the American occupation zone alone, 13.4 million adults were required to complete a 131-question political screening form called the Fragebogen. Every German who wanted to hold public office, teach, practice law, or work in media had to fill one out. The responses were used to classify people into five categories: Major Offenders, Offenders, Lesser Offenders, Followers, and Exonerated. This sounds thorough. In practice, it was overwhelmed by its own ambitions.
        </p>

        <p>
          General Dwight Eisenhower initially estimated denazification would take fifty years. He was closer to right than the politicians who followed. By 1947, Cold War priorities had shifted the calculus entirely. The United States needed a functioning West Germany as a bulwark against Soviet expansion, and functioning West Germany required the administrative class that had run things under Hitler. Responsibility for denazification was quietly transferred from Allied military courts to German civilian tribunals, which were far more lenient. A fast-track procedure adopted in 1948 processed former Nazi Party members on paperwork alone, rubber-stamping exonerations. In 95 percent of cases, tribunals classified subjects as "Followers" or "Exonerated." Only 3,600 people in the entire US zone were classified as "Major Offenders."
        </p>

        <p>
          The Cold War did not merely slow denazification. It reversed it. The 1951 Article 131 law allowed the reinstatement of former civil servants who had been dismissed during denazification. Former Nazis returned to the judiciary, the foreign ministry, the intelligence services, and the police. Konrad Adenauer's chief of staff, Hans Globke, had co-authored the legal commentary on the Nuremberg Race Laws. The head of West German foreign intelligence, Reinhard Gehlen, had been a Wehrmacht general on the Eastern Front. These were not isolated cases. They were the system.
        </p>

        <p>
          What actually worked in Germany was not denazification. It was time, economics, and generational change. The Wirtschaftswunder, the economic miracle of the 1950s and 1960s, gave ordinary Germans a material stake in the new democratic order. The Basic Law, West Germany's constitution promulgated in 1949, created institutional safeguards, an entrenched bill of rights, a strong constitutional court, a constructive vote of no confidence, that made democratic backsliding structurally difficult. And the student protest movements of the late 1960s forced a genuine reckoning with the Nazi past that the parent generation had been eager to avoid.
        </p>

        <p>
          The timeline matters. Germany held its first democratic election in 1949, four years after the war ended. Most historians place the consolidation of genuine democratic culture at the late 1960s, when Willy Brandt's election as Chancellor marked a full generational shift. That is twenty to twenty-five years from the fall of Nazism to a functioning democratic culture. A generation. Not a trial, not a program, not a policy. A generation of people growing up in a new system, attending reformed schools, and eventually outnumbering the people who remembered what it was like before.
        </p>

        <p>
          The education reform deserves particular attention because it is the mechanism most relevant to the current American situation. The Nazi regime had thoroughly Nazified German education over 12 years. After liberation, all Nazi-approved textbooks were immediately banned. But the challenge was acute: teachers who had not belonged to the Nazi Party were extremely scarce. The Allies implemented emergency retraining programs, some lasting only four to eight months, and in the Soviet zone approximately 40,000 new teachers were rapidly trained and deployed. In the western zones, some teachers with Nazi ties were allowed to return after completing what were grimly called "Entbraunungskursen," or "de-browning courses." The curriculum itself was rebuilt from scratch, and over the following decades, German schools developed some of the world's most rigorous education about the Holocaust and the mechanisms of authoritarian persuasion.
        </p>

        <p>
          The result is visible today. Germany has some of the strongest legal and cultural taboos against authoritarian movements of any country in the world. The Basic Law allows the banning of political parties that seek to undermine the democratic order, a power that has been used twice and was invoked again against the Alternative for Germany (Alternative fur Deutschland, AfD) party in January 2024. Germans who display Nazi symbols face criminal prosecution. Holocaust denial is a criminal offense. These are not merely laws. They reflect a culture that was deliberately and painstakingly constructed over a generation, through education, through public monuments and museums, through an ongoing national conversation about collective responsibility. It did not happen naturally. It was built.
        </p>

        <p>
          The American parallel is both encouraging and discouraging. Encouraging because it shows that democratic culture can be constructed intentionally. Discouraging because America has never had a comparable reckoning with its own history of intolerance. There is no American equivalent of Germany's culture of Vergangenheitsbewaltigung, the "coming to terms with the past." The Civil War ended 161 years ago, and significant portions of the American South still celebrate the Confederacy. The civil rights movement achieved legal equality 60 years ago, and structural racism remains embedded in housing, education, policing, and the criminal justice system. Reconstruction was America's one serious attempt at post-authoritarian accountability, and it was abandoned after only 12 years in favor of a deal that restored white supremacist governance throughout the South. The Compromise of 1877 was America's Pact of Forgetting, and the consequences lasted for nearly a century of Jim Crow.
        </p>

        <h2>How South Africa Exited</h2>

        <p>
          South Africa took a radically different path. When apartheid ended in 1994 after negotiations between the African National Congress (ANC) and the white-minority government, the country faced the same fundamental question Germany had faced: what do you do with the perpetrators? South Africa's answer was the Truth and Reconciliation Commission (TRC), established in 1996 and chaired by Archbishop Desmond Tutu.
        </p>

        <p>
          The TRC's design was built on a philosophical premise that remains controversial: that truth could substitute for punishment. Perpetrators of gross human rights violations could apply for amnesty from both criminal and civil prosecution. The sole requirement was full disclosure. You had to tell the complete truth about what you had done, and the acts had to have been politically motivated. If you told the truth, you went free. If you lied or stayed silent, you could be prosecuted.
        </p>

        <p>
          The numbers reveal the outcome. The TRC received 7,112 amnesty applications. It granted amnesty to 849 applicants, roughly 12 percent. It denied amnesty to 5,392. It collected approximately 21,000 victim statements and held around 2,000 public hearings. The Commission's Final Report recommended reparations for victims and prosecution for those denied amnesty when evidence existed.
        </p>

        <p>
          Neither recommendation was meaningfully implemented. The government largely failed to deliver the reparations the TRC recommended. And perpetrators who were denied amnesty were almost never prosecuted afterward. The result was something critics describe as de facto blanket amnesty. The TRC created a comprehensive historical record of apartheid atrocities and gave victims a public forum to tell their stories. These are not nothing. But they are not justice in any sense that the word ordinarily carries. The Nelson Mandela Foundation's 2024 assessment, titled "Failing South Africa: the Truth Commission 30 years on," concluded that the promise of the TRC remained largely unfulfilled.
        </p>

        <p>
          The fundamental tension is irreconcilable. The TRC operated on a philosophy of restorative justice, concerned with healing relationships and building a new social contract, rather than retributive justice focused on punishment. Restorative justice is not a euphemism for letting people off the hook. It is a coherent philosophical framework with deep roots in indigenous African traditions. But for the millions of Black South Africans who had been brutalized under apartheid, who had family members tortured and killed by agents of the state, being told that the path to reconciliation was to watch your torturer confess on television and then walk free felt less like healing and more like a second violation.
        </p>

        <h2>How Argentina Exited</h2>

        <p>
          Argentina's journey is the most dramatic of all the case studies, and in some ways the most hopeful, though that hope requires patience measured in decades.
        </p>

        <p>
          The military junta that ruled Argentina from 1976 to 1983, in what became known as the Dirty War, conducted systematic state terrorism. An estimated 22,000 to 30,000 people were killed or "disappeared," a verb the Argentine military turned into a transitive one. The disappeared were kidnapped, tortured, and murdered, often by being drugged and dropped from aircraft into the Rio de la Plata estuary. Five hundred babies were stolen from imprisoned mothers and given to military families. The junta collapsed after economic crisis and the catastrophic Falklands War against Britain in 1982.
        </p>

        <p>
          What followed was the Trial of the Juntas in 1985, the first time in Latin American history that a civilian democratic government prosecuted its own former military dictators. The trial was Argentina's Nuremberg. Lead prosecutor Julio Cesar Strassera, whose young assistant Luis Moreno Ocampo would later become the first chief prosecutor of the International Criminal Court (ICC), presented 833 witnesses over 530 hours. Nine members of the three successive juntas were tried. Five were convicted, including life sentences for Jorge Rafael Videla and Emilio Massera, the commanders of the first junta.
        </p>

        <p>
          Then the backlash came. Military pressure led to the 1986 "Full Stop Law," which set a 60-day deadline for new prosecutions. The 1987 "Due Obedience Law" exempted everyone below the rank of lieutenant colonel, presuming they were following orders. And in 1989-1990, President Carlos Menem pardoned all convicted junta members, including Videla and Massera. Justice had been won, and then dismantled, in the space of five years.
        </p>

        <p>
          But Argentina's story did not end there. In 2003, President Nestor Kirchner's government began reversing the amnesty. Congress repealed the Full Stop Law. In 2005, the Supreme Court ruled both amnesty laws unconstitutional, reopening all cases. In 2006-2012, Menem's pardons were struck down. Videla was convicted again in 2010 and sentenced to life. In 2012, he received 50 years for systematic baby theft.
        </p>

        <p>
          As of 2024, more than 3,161 people have been charged with crimes against humanity in Argentina, and over 1,200 have been convicted. Trials remain ongoing. This is, by a wide margin, the most comprehensive accountability process for state terror in Latin American history, and one of the most thorough anywhere in the world. But it took forty years. The junta fell in 1983. The first real wave of accountability did not begin until 2005. The arc of justice in Argentina is not short. It spans a human lifetime.
        </p>

        <h2>How Spain Exited</h2>

        <p>
          And then there is Spain, which represents the opposite extreme from Argentina: a successful transition to stable democracy achieved through complete impunity for the prior regime.
        </p>

        <p>
          Francisco Franco ruled Spain as a fascist dictator for 40 years, from 1939 to 1975. His regime killed an estimated 100,000 to 200,000 people during and after the Spanish Civil War. An estimated 114,000 victims remain in unmarked mass graves across the country. Only Cambodia has more mass graves, according to Amnesty International.
        </p>

        <p>
          When Franco died in 1975, Spain's political class, both left and right, entered into what became known as the Pacto del Olvido, the Pact of Forgetting. This was not a formal document but an informal consensus to avoid confronting the Franco regime's crimes. Its legal expression was the 1977 Amnesty Law, passed by the first democratically elected parliament. The law released political prisoners who had resisted Franco. It simultaneously shielded all Francoist officials from prosecution for human rights violations. It made accountability legally impossible.
        </p>

        <p>
          The result was zero prosecutions. Zero exhumations. Zero removal of Francoist memorials or street names. No truth commission. No lustration, which is the process of screening former regime officials from public positions. Franco's appointees continued serving in government, the judiciary, the military, and the police without interruption. The country's transition to democracy was shepherded by King Juan Carlos I, who had been personally appointed by Franco as his successor. The pragmatic logic was straightforward: Spain's memory of the Civil War, in which half a million people died, made everyone afraid of what accountability might unleash. The cost of remembering, they believed, was greater than the cost of forgetting.
        </p>

        <p>
          Spain became a stable, prosperous democracy. It joined the European Union in 1986. Its Freedom House score today is 94. By every conventional measure, the transition worked. But "worked" is doing enormous moral heavy lifting in that sentence. The 114,000 dead in mass graves remain largely unexhumed. It was not until 2007 that the Historical Memory Law formally condemned the Franco regime. The 2022 Democratic Memory Law went further, declaring the regime illegal and making the state responsible for exhuming victims. But even that law did not repeal the 1977 Amnesty Law. As of 2024, no Francoist official has ever been prosecuted for human rights violations. The dead are still waiting.
        </p>

        <p>
          The Spanish case matters for Americans because it demonstrates something that the German case, with its occupation forces and Nuremberg Trials, can obscure: transitions from within are different from transitions imposed from outside. No foreign army will occupy the United States and impose denazification. If American democracy recovers from the MAGA era, it will be a transition negotiated among Americans, by Americans, within existing American institutions. And the Spanish model suggests that transitions from within almost always involve some version of the Pact of Forgetting. The people who hold power under the old regime do not surrender it unconditionally. They negotiate terms. And the terms almost always include some form of immunity, whether explicit, as in Spain's 1977 Amnesty Law, or implicit, as in the American tradition of not prosecuting former presidents, which the Ford pardon of Nixon established and which every subsequent administration has largely honored until the Trump era.
        </p>

        <h2>The Accountability Scorecard</h2>

        <p>
          When you place these case studies side by side, the pattern is clear and deeply uncomfortable for anyone who believes in justice.
        </p>

        <Chart chartId="tolerance-accountability" title="Conviction Rates in Post-Authoritarian Transitions" caption="Rwanda's Gacaca community courts processed 1.96 million cases with an 86% conviction rate but faced serious due process concerns. Argentina's 1,200+ convictions over 40 years represent the high-water mark for conventional legal accountability. Germany, South Africa, and Spain each demonstrate different flavors of impunity." />

        <p class="source-note">Sources: International Center for Transitional Justice; Human Rights Watch (Rwanda Gacaca report); Mary Fulbrook (Germany); South Africa TRC Final Report; Spanish Democratic Memory Law documentation.</p>

        <p>
          Rwanda is the outlier, and its outlier status is instructive. After the 1994 genocide, in which approximately 800,000 Tutsi were killed in a hundred days, the Rwandan government faced an impossible situation: hundreds of thousands of perpetrators and a destroyed judicial system. The solution was the Gacaca courts, community-based tribunals that processed an astonishing 1,958,634 cases with an 86 percent conviction rate. That is justice at a scale no other country has attempted. But it came at a cost that the International Center for Transitional Justice and Human Rights Watch have extensively documented: limited defense rights, community pressure to convict, and a process that many outside observers considered closer to collective judgment than individual justice.
        </p>

        <p>
          Argentina stands as the high-water mark for conventional legal accountability, with more than 1,200 convictions over four decades. But even in Argentina, the convicted represent a small fraction of the thousands who participated in state terrorism. Germany convicted approximately 100,000 people across all courts worldwide, which sounds significant until you remember that 8.5 million people were Nazi Party members. South Africa granted amnesty to 849 people and prosecuted almost no one. Spain prosecuted exactly no one.
        </p>

        <p>
          The structural reality is this: in every historical case of mass atrocity or authoritarian rule, fewer than one percent of perpetrators face legal consequences. This is not because justice systems are lazy or corrupt, though they sometimes are. It is because the scale of mass participation in state violence fundamentally exceeds the capacity of any legal system to process. You cannot try millions of people. You cannot even try hundreds of thousands. What you can do is try a symbolic handful, hope that the symbolism matters, and build institutions that make recurrence harder. That is the ceiling of what transitional justice has ever achieved, anywhere, at any time in human history.
        </p>

        <p>
          There is a deeper reason why accountability is so rare, and it goes beyond logistics. Transitional moments, the periods when authoritarian regimes fall, are inherently unstable. The incoming democratic government faces a dilemma that political scientists call the "justice cascade": pursue aggressive accountability and risk destabilizing the fragile new democracy, or offer amnesty and risk undermining the legitimacy of the new order. In Argentina, the military's response to the Trial of the Juntas was not resignation but threats. The Easter rebellions of 1987, in which military officers revolted against the government, were directly caused by the prosecutions. Alfonsin's government passed the Due Obedience Law not out of cowardice but out of a calculation that continued prosecutions would provoke a military coup. He may have been right. The calculation was morally repugnant and politically defensible, a combination that turns out to be the norm in transitional justice.
        </p>

        <p>
          In Spain, the Pact of Forgetting was not a failure of moral imagination. It was a cold assessment of power. Franco's military was intact. His security services were intact. His judiciary was intact. The transition was being guided by Franco's own designated successor. Under those conditions, pushing for accountability meant risking a second civil war in a country that had not yet recovered from the first. The Spanish left, including the Communist Party under Santiago Carrillo, made the conscious decision that a functioning democracy without justice was preferable to a collapsed democracy with it. History has largely vindicated that calculation in institutional terms, even as it leaves 114,000 bodies in unmarked graves. The moral weight of that trade-off cannot be resolved by data. But the data can tell us that the trade-off exists, that it has been made in every transitional case, and that there is no case in recorded history where a new democracy chose aggressive accountability without paying a significant price for it.
        </p>

        <p>
          This pattern should inform how Americans think about the post-MAGA future. The people who staffed the current administration, the officials who wrote the executive orders, the judges who were appointed, the bureaucrats who implemented family separations, the ICE agents who conducted raids, the lawyers who crafted the legal justifications, they will not disappear when the administration changes. They will still be in the military, the judiciary, the civil service, the private sector, and the political system. Some of them will have broken laws and can be prosecuted. Most will have operated within the broad discretion that American law grants to executive branch officials. The question will not be whether to pursue justice. The question will be how much justice a new democracy can pursue without empowering the backlash that created the problem in the first place.
        </p>

        <h2>What Recovery Looks Like</h2>

        <p>
          If accountability is always incomplete, what about recovery itself? Here the historical record offers both hope and caution.
        </p>

        <Chart chartId="tolerance-recovery" title="Democratic Recovery: Before and After Transition" caption="V-Dem Liberal Democracy Index scores (0-1) before and after major democratic transitions. All five countries achieved dramatic improvement, though none reached the levels of established democracies like the Nordics (0.90+). V-Dem scores are constructed from expert surveys of 3,700+ country specialists." />

        <p class="source-note">Sources: V-Dem Institute, Democracy Report 2025; Freedom House annual reports; V-Dem Liberal Democracy Index historical data.</p>

        <p>
          The chart shows that democratic recovery is real. Portugal went from a V-Dem Liberal Democracy score of 0.05 under the Estado Novo to 0.90 today, among the most successful transitions in history. Spain rose from 0.08 under Franco to 0.85. South Korea, Argentina, and South Africa all show dramatic improvement. These transitions are genuine and they matter. People who lived under dictatorship now live in something meaningfully closer to freedom.
        </p>

        <p>
          But recent research injects a heavy dose of realism. A study published in January 2026 by University of Birmingham professor Nic Cheeseman and colleagues, covered by NPR under the headline "After backsliding, democracy often comes back weaker and more fragile," analyzed three decades of data and found that 90 percent of democratic "U-turns" do not last. Most countries that recover from authoritarian backsliding cannot sustain democracy for even five consecutive years. Even when recovery occurs, the resulting democracy is significantly weaker than it was before. Authoritarian loyalists embedded in courts, bureaucracies, and political parties continue undermining accountability long after the authoritarian leader is gone.
        </p>

        <p>
          The Carnegie Endowment for International Peace published an extensive comparative analysis in 2025 studying recovery in Poland, Brazil, Zambia, and Senegal. Their findings are sobering: "few, if any, 'recovering' backsliders have regained the level of democratic quality they had achieved prior to the backsliding episode." The damage, in other words, appears to be permanent. Or at least longer-lasting than any recovery currently on record.
        </p>

        <p>
          Carnegie identified two factors that proved most essential for defeating backsliding leaders: civil society resilience, meaning organizations that effectively inform citizens about democratic damage, and smart opposition tactics, meaning coalitions that broaden their appeal beyond traditional supporters. Critically, the research found that "judicial systems only inconsistently served as bulwarks." Courts alone cannot save democracy. Civic and political mobilization are the primary mechanisms of recovery.
        </p>

        <p>
          This finding should shape how tolerant people think about strategy. If you are counting on the courts to stop democratic erosion, you are relying on a mechanism that has failed more often than it has succeeded. If you are counting on opposition politicians to save the day, you are relying on a mechanism that works only when those politicians build coalitions beyond their base. The mechanism with the best track record is civil society: independent media, nonprofit organizations, community groups, labor unions, professional associations, and informal networks of citizens who refuse to normalize what should not be normal.
        </p>

        <h2>What Will Happen in the United States</h2>

        <p>
          Now let me attempt the hardest part of this essay: a clear-eyed assessment of what is likely to happen, not what I want to happen.
        </p>

        <p>
          The United States has several structural advantages that most backsliding democracies lack. Its per capita Gross Domestic Product (GDP) of $89,678 makes it, by far, the wealthiest country to face serious democratic breakdown. Wealthy democracies are historically more resilient because citizens have more to lose from instability and more resources to organize resistance. Its elections are administered by states, not the federal government, creating a decentralized system that is much harder for any single actor to capture. Its civil society participation score on V-Dem is 0.98, among the highest in the world, compared to Hungary's 0.44. Its constitutional amendment process is extraordinarily difficult, creating structural barriers to permanent autocratic consolidation.
        </p>

        <p>
          But the United States also faces headwinds that are genuinely unprecedented. No country of this size, wealth, and institutional complexity has undergone the kind of rapid democratic erosion the US is experiencing. The Century Foundation's Democracy Meter documented a 28 percent decline in democratic quality in a single year. The Toda Peace Institute's Robert Kaufman warns of a genuine risk of "competitive authoritarianism," a system where democratic facades persist but power cannot be held accountable. And the Cheeseman study's finding that 90 percent of democratic recoveries fail should make anyone who tells you "the institutions will hold" think carefully about what "hold" means and for how long.
        </p>

        <p>
          Here is what I believe the data supports as the most likely trajectory, and I want to be honest that I find it discouraging.
        </p>

        <p>
          First, the MAGA movement will eventually lose political dominance. All authoritarian movements eventually do. The question is whether that happens through electoral defeat, internal collapse, demographic shift, or some combination. Trump is 79 years old. Authoritarian movements built around a single personality tend to fracture when that personality exits. The More in Common research institute, which conducted the most detailed segmentation of Trump's 2024 coalition, found that his voters break into four distinct types: MAGA Hardliners at 29 percent of Trump voters, Anti-Woke Conservatives at 21 percent, Mainline Republicans at 30 percent, and the Reluctant Right at 20 percent. Only the first category, roughly 22 million people, are ideologically committed to the movement. The other three groups are a coalition of convenience that is held together by a single personality and a shared set of grievances. When that personality exits the stage, the coalition will fracture. Not because people will change their minds, but because the thing holding them together will be gone.
        </p>

        <p>
          But "eventually" is doing a lot of work in that sentence. "Eventually" could mean 2028 or it could mean 2040. In Hungary, Viktor Orban has maintained power since 2010 and shows no sign of losing it. In Turkey, Recep Tayyip Erdogan has held power since 2003. In Russia, Vladimir Putin has ruled in some capacity since 1999, making his tenure longer than many dictatorships of the twentieth century. Authoritarians can be remarkably durable when they capture enough institutional levers, and the current administration is capturing them at a pace that even V-Dem's experts have described as "the fastest evolving episode of autocratization the USA has been through in modern history." The structural advantages I mentioned earlier, wealth, decentralized elections, strong civil society, make eventual recovery likely. They do not make it fast. And every year of delay compounds the institutional damage.
        </p>

        <p>
          Second, when the MAGA era ends, accountability will be minimal. History is unambiguous on this point. The most likely outcome is something between Spain's total amnesia and Argentina's slow-burning prosecution of a symbolic handful. There will be no Nuremberg. There will be no mass trials. Most of the officials who implemented family separations, who weaponized federal agencies, who undermined electoral integrity, will face no consequences. Some will write memoirs. Some will become lobbyists. Some will run for office again under different branding. This is not cynicism. This is what the data from every prior case of democratic recovery tells us will happen. The Argentine model, where accountability takes 40 years and eventually produces over a thousand convictions, is the best-case scenario. The Spanish model, where accountability never comes at all, is the most common scenario. America will fall somewhere between the two, and closer to Spain than to Argentina, because the American system does not have the constitutional mechanisms or the political will for the kind of sustained prosecution that Argentina achieved.
        </p>

        <p>
          Third, the democracy that emerges on the other side will be weaker than the one that preceded the crisis. This is Carnegie's finding across every case they studied, and there is no reason to think the US will be an exception. Institutional damage accumulates. Norms, once broken, are not easily rebuilt. The people who staffed the authoritarian project will remain in the judiciary, in the bureaucracy, in state governments, in corporate boardrooms. They will not disappear. They will adapt, rebrand, and wait. The project of strengthening democracy after the MAGA era will take a generation, just as it did in Germany.
        </p>

        <p>
          Fourth, and this is where I want to dwell, the work that matters most is happening now, not later. Carnegie's research is clear: prevention is significantly more effective than restoration. Every percentage point of democratic quality preserved today is worth more than a percentage point clawed back after the damage is done. The organizations fighting voter suppression today, the journalists documenting abuses today, the lawyers challenging unconstitutional actions today, the local officials refusing to comply with unlawful orders today, they are doing the most consequential work in this entire struggle. They are the 10 percent that makes recovery possible.
        </p>

        <h2>What Boots-on-the-Ground Justice Actually Looks Like</h2>

        <p>
          If the historical pattern holds, here is what post-MAGA justice in the United States will probably look like. I offer this not as what I want, but as what the data suggests we should expect and plan for.
        </p>

        <p>
          There will be a handful of high-profile prosecutions. Maybe a few dozen. These will target people who committed clearly documentable crimes: officials who ordered unlawful actions, who destroyed evidence, who personally profited from corruption. They will take years. Many will fail. Some will result in pardons by subsequent sympathetic administrations, just as they did in Argentina. The number of people who face any legal consequence will be vanishingly small relative to the millions who supported the movement.
        </p>

        <p>
          There may be a commission of some kind. Not a criminal tribunal but an investigative body, similar in spirit to the South African TRC or the Argentine CONADEP (Comision Nacional sobre la Desaparicion de Personas, National Commission on the Disappearance of Persons). It will document what happened: the family separations, the weaponization of federal agencies, the attempts to overturn elections, the corruption. It will produce a report. The report will be devastating. Half the country will not read it. The other half already knew. Commissions are important for the historical record but their immediate political impact is typically modest.
        </p>

        <p>
          There will be institutional reforms. These are actually the most consequential outcome, even though they generate the least emotional satisfaction. After Watergate, Congress passed the Ethics in Government Act, the Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Act (FISA), and amendments to the Federal Election Campaign Act. After the MAGA era, there will likely be reforms to presidential pardon power, the independence of the Department of Justice (DOJ), the enforcement mechanisms of congressional subpoenas, and the guardrails around emergency powers. These reforms will be imperfect. They will not prevent the next authoritarian from trying. But they will raise the cost of trying, and in democratic systems, marginal costs matter.
        </p>

        <p>
          There will be no mass accountability for ordinary supporters. The 77 million people who voted for Trump in 2024 will not face consequences for their vote. Nor should they. The right to vote for terrible candidates is inseparable from the right to vote at all. Democracy's central wager is that the electorate will, over time, choose well. When it does not, the remedy is more democracy, not less. This is infuriating to anyone who has watched what those votes enabled. It is also the only framework consistent with the tolerance we claim to value.
        </p>

        <p>
          It is worth pausing on why this last point matters so much. There is a strong temptation among tolerant people to engage in what might be called social denazification: the impulse to shun, fire, defriend, and ostracize anyone who voted for Trump. The impulse is understandable. It is also counterproductive. The data from every successful democratic recovery shows that the path to restoration runs through the moderate periphery of the authoritarian coalition, not through its hardline core. More in Common's research tells us that 70 percent of Trump's voters are not ideological MAGA supporters. They are conservatives, reluctant Republicans, and people who voted on single issues like the economy or immigration. Treating all 77 million as irredeemable enemies is not just morally questionable, it is strategically stupid. It pushes the persuadable periphery deeper into the arms of the hardliners, exactly the dynamic that authoritarian leaders need to maintain their coalition. In Poland, the opposition won in 2023 by peeling away moderate PiS voters. In Brazil, Lula won by appealing to centrists who had voted for Bolsonaro in 2018. The math is clear: you cannot build a winning coalition for tolerance by writing off a third of the electorate.
        </p>

        <p>
          The real justice will be generational. Just as Germany needed twenty-five years and a new generation to develop genuine democratic culture, the United States will need time for the political coalition that enabled MAGA to age out of dominance and for younger voters who grew up in the wreckage to build something different. This is not a satisfying answer. It means that the people who caused the damage will largely escape consequences, and that the people who inherit the damage will bear the burden of repair. It means that tolerance, as a political project, is not a sprint or even a marathon but a relay race in which each generation can only advance the baton so far.
        </p>

        <h2>What Tolerant People Should Actually Do</h2>

        <p>
          I started this essay angry. I am still angry. But the numbers have changed the texture of my anger. Before, it was diffuse, a cloud of moral outrage directed at 77 million people I cannot persuade and cannot punish. Now it is something more focused.
        </p>

        <p>
          The numbers tell me that I have been thinking about this wrong. I was thinking in terms of defeating intolerance, as though it were an enemy that could be routed on a battlefield. The historical record says that intolerant movements are not defeated in the way we imagine. They are not eradicated. They are not brought to justice. They are not held to account. They are outlasted. They are made obsolete. They are replaced by institutions and cultures that make their return marginally harder. And even then, they come back in new forms. Always. In every country. Without exception.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is what Carnegie means when they say prevention is more effective than restoration. You do not build tolerance by punishing intolerance after the fact. You build tolerance by making it the default condition of the institutions people live within. Schools that teach critical thinking. Media that maintains independence. Courts that enforce rights. Communities that normalize diversity. Electoral systems that make participation easy and manipulation hard. None of these are glamorous. None of them satisfy the desire for retribution. All of them work better than the alternatives.
        </p>

        <p>
          The numbers also tell me something about the scale of the work. There are 77 million people on the other side. I cannot convert them. I cannot convince them. I probably cannot even talk to most of them in any productive way. But I can support institutions that outlast them. I can support journalism that documents what is happening. I can support legal organizations that challenge unlawful actions. I can support voter protection groups that defend the electoral process. I can support local organizations that build bridges across the divides that authoritarianism exploits. Every dollar, every hour, every conversation directed at these institutions is a marginal increase in the probability of recovery. And the data says that marginal increases are the only kind that exist.
        </p>

        <p>
          Let me be specific about what this means in practice, because vagueness is the enemy of action. The Carnegie research identified civil society as the primary mechanism of democratic recovery in every successful case. Civil society is not one thing. It is an ecosystem of independent organizations that serve as a check on state power and as an infrastructure for collective action. In the United States, this ecosystem includes legal organizations like the American Civil Liberties Union (ACLU), the Lawyers' Committee for Civil Rights, and state-level legal aid societies that challenge unconstitutional executive actions in court. It includes press organizations like ProPublica, the Marshall Project, and local investigative newsrooms that document what the government is doing. It includes voter protection organizations like the League of Women Voters, Common Cause, and the state-level organizations that train poll watchers and challenge voter suppression. It includes community organizations that serve immigrant communities, that provide bail funds, that connect people facing deportation with legal representation.
        </p>

        <p>
          None of these organizations, individually, will save American democracy. That is not what the data says. What the data says is that the aggregate effect of thousands of organizations, millions of volunteers, and billions of dollars directed at preserving democratic infrastructure is the single strongest predictor of whether a backsliding democracy recovers. It is stronger than court rulings, which Carnegie found to be "inconsistent" as bulwarks. It is stronger than opposition party strategy, though that matters too. It is stronger than international pressure, economic sanctions, or moral exhortation. The boring, unglamorous, daily work of maintaining the institutions that make democracy possible is the most effective tool the tolerant have ever had.
        </p>

        <p>
          The research also points to something counterintuitive about coalition building. The successful recoveries in Poland, Brazil, Zambia, and Senegal all involved opposition movements that broadened their appeal beyond their natural base. In Poland, the opposition to the Law and Justice Party's (Prawo i Sprawiedliwosc, PiS) democratic backsliding succeeded in 2023 by building a coalition that included centrists, liberals, and even some conservatives who opposed the erosion of judicial independence. In Brazil, the movement that defeated Jair Bolsonaro in 2022 brought together labor, environmentalists, indigenous rights groups, and center-right voters who simply wanted democratic stability. The common thread is that winning coalitions were not ideologically pure. They were strategically broad.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is uncomfortable for many tolerant people because it means working alongside people you disagree with on many issues. It means accepting that the person who opposes MAGA's assault on the judiciary might also oppose your position on healthcare or taxation or immigration. Building coalitions broad enough to restore democracy requires tolerating disagreement within the coalition, which is itself an exercise in the very tolerance we are trying to defend. The irony is not lost on me.
        </p>

        <p>
          Finally, the data says something important about where individual effort has the highest return. National politics captures most of our attention, but local institutions are where democratic resilience is built or eroded. School boards that adopt curricula teaching critical thinking and media literacy. City councils that pass policies protecting immigrant communities. State legislatures that maintain election integrity. County officials who refuse to comply with unconstitutional federal orders. Judges who uphold the rule of law against executive overreach. These are the thousands of small decisions that, in aggregate, determine whether a democracy survives or falls. They are the domain where a single engaged citizen can make a measurable difference. You cannot, by yourself, change the direction of national politics. But you can change the direction of your school board, your city council, your local bar association, your church, your neighborhood. And the data says that is where the real work happens.
        </p>

        <p>
          I also want to be honest about something the numbers forced me to confront. I said at the beginning that I want justice. The numbers tell me that justice, in the retributive sense, is a fantasy. It has never been achieved at scale after any period of mass intolerance, anywhere, at any time in human history. Not in Germany. Not in South Africa. Not in Argentina. Not in Spain. Not in Rwanda. The maximum accountability any society has ever achieved is prosecuting a tiny fraction of perpetrators, usually decades after the fact, while the vast majority live out their lives in peace. If justice means holding every participant accountable, then justice has never existed and never will exist.
        </p>

        <p>
          But if justice means building a society where the things that were done cannot easily be done again, then justice is possible. Imperfect, incomplete, infuriatingly slow, but possible. That is the lesson of every democratic transition in the historical record. The tolerant do not win by defeating the intolerant. They win by building things that last longer than any movement of intolerance can sustain itself. They win by being more patient than their adversaries, which is the most difficult kind of courage, the kind that does not look like courage at all.
        </p>

        <p>
          There is a distinction worth drawing here between patience and passivity. Patience means understanding that the project of restoring and strengthening tolerance is generational work. It means accepting that you will not see the full fruits of your efforts in your lifetime, just as the denazifiers of 1945 did not live to see the Germany of 2024. Passivity means doing nothing and hoping it works out. The data is absolutely clear that passivity does not work. The 90 percent of democratic recoveries that fail, the ones Cheeseman documented, are the ones where citizens assumed that institutions would save themselves, that norms would reassert themselves, that the pendulum would naturally swing back. It does not swing back on its own. It swings back because people push it.
        </p>

        <p>
          I am still angry. I think the anger is appropriate. Seventy-seven million people voted for cruelty, and the institutions of American democracy are crumbling. Children are being separated from their parents. People are being detained for the crime of existing. Courts are being captured. Federal agencies are being weaponized. These are not abstractions. They are happening to real people right now, and the people doing them will almost certainly never be held accountable.
        </p>

        <p>
          But anger without strategy is just noise. And the numbers point toward a strategy, even if it is not the one I wanted. Support institutions. Protect the electoral process. Build coalitions broader than your comfort zone. Work locally where your effort has the highest marginal return. Play the long game. Understand that the arc of justice, if it bends at all, bends over generations, not news cycles. And understand that the 10 percent of democratic recoveries that succeed are the ones where people did all of these things, not because it felt heroic, but because the data said it was the only thing that worked.
        </p>

        <p>
          One last thought. I wrote this essay because I wanted the numbers to make me feel better. They did not. They made me feel more clear-eyed, which is different. Clarity is not comfort. But it is the prerequisite for action that actually matters.
        </p>

        <p>
          I keep coming back to the Germany timeline. Twenty to twenty-five years from the fall of the Nazis to a consolidated democratic culture. If you were a young German in 1945, watching your country lie in ruins, you spent your entire young adulthood building something you would not see completed until middle age. The people who laid the foundations of German democracy in the 1940s and 1950s did not live to see the student movements of the 1960s that finally forced the reckoning. The people who wrote the Basic Law in 1949 could not have predicted that it would produce one of the world's most robust democracies by 1990. They built anyway. Not because they could see the outcome, but because the alternative was to do nothing and watch something worse grow in the rubble.
        </p>

        <p>
          That is the math of tolerance. It is not inspiring in the way we want it to be. It does not offer the satisfaction of defeating your enemies or the catharsis of watching the guilty punished. It offers only the hard, slow, incremental work of building institutions that are slightly more resilient than the forces that seek to destroy them. And it asks you to do that work without knowing whether it will succeed, without receiving credit for it, and without the certainty that the people who come after you will continue what you started.
        </p>

        <p>
          If this essay has made you more clear-eyed, even if it has not made you more hopeful, then it has done its job. The numbers are not on our side. They never have been. But the numbers also say that tolerance, fragile and imperfect, has survived every period of intolerance that has ever ended. Not because the tolerant were stronger. But because they were still there when it was over.
        </p>

